249
00:11:31,682 --> 00:11:36,990
They found the Donald Trump
neuron in a CLIP model,

250
00:11:36,990 --> 00:11:38,970
for example.

251
00:11:38,970 --> 00:11:42,240
But it turns out that
in language models when

252
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:45,150
you do this, you just
say which sentences

253
00:11:45,150 --> 00:11:48,430
cause this neuron to activate.

254
00:11:48,430 --> 00:11:52,360
The answer doesn't
make that much sense.

255
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:56,710
So here's a visualization
of a bunch of example texts

256
00:11:56,710 --> 00:11:58,730
for which a neuron
in a model activates.

257
00:11:58,730 --> 00:12:04,370
And there's just a lot of stuff.

258
00:12:04,370 --> 00:12:11,560
There's code and some Chinese
and some math and hemlock

259
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:12,173
for Socrates.

260
00:12:12,173 --> 00:12:15,340

261
00:12:15,340 --> 00:12:16,850
It's not especially clear.

262
00:12:16,850 --> 00:12:19,580
And of course, there's no reason
it would need to be, right.

263
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:21,103
It's just learned a function.

264
00:12:21,103 --> 00:12:23,020
And so asking for a
neuron to be interpretable

265
00:12:23,020 --> 00:12:25,150
is like a bit of a Hail Mary.

266
00:12:25,150 --> 00:12:27,680
And it's pretty cool
that works sometimes,

267
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:31,060
but it's not
particularly systematic.

268
00:12:31,060 --> 00:12:35,030
So there's a prior--

269
00:12:35,030 --> 00:12:39,405
a prior from neuroscience
is that maybe while there's

270
00:12:39,405 --> 00:12:40,780
a whole bunch of
neurons going on

271
00:12:40,780 --> 00:12:42,322
or something that
in any given moment

272
00:12:42,322 --> 00:12:45,720
maybe the model is not thinking
of that many things at once.

273
00:12:45,720 --> 00:12:48,290
Maybe there is some
sparsity here where

274
00:12:48,290 --> 00:12:51,060
if there were a map of the
concepts the model is using

275
00:12:51,060 --> 00:12:53,540
or the subroutines
it's using or something

276
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:58,280
that on any given token, it's
only using a few at a time.

277
00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:02,270
And that's just maybe
a slightly better guess

278
00:13:02,270 --> 00:13:04,020
than maybe the neurons
are interpretable.

279
00:13:04,020 --> 00:13:05,670
It's not necessarily
a great prior,

280
00:13:05,670 --> 00:13:07,212
but it's something
you can work with.

281
00:13:07,212 --> 00:13:11,810
And so you can fit linear
combinations of neurons

282
00:13:11,810 --> 00:13:16,070
such that each activation
vector is a sparse combination

283
00:13:16,070 --> 00:13:17,700
of these dictionary elements.

284
00:13:17,700 --> 00:13:20,450
This is called dictionary
learning classical ML.

285
00:13:20,450 --> 00:13:22,080
And we just did it.

286
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:24,230
So you just gather a
bunch of activations

287
00:13:24,230 --> 00:13:26,415
from the model when you put
through a trillion texts

288
00:13:26,415 --> 00:13:28,290
or something and then
you take those vectors,

289
00:13:28,290 --> 00:13:30,020
you look for
dictionaries and then

290
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:32,460
you look at when are those
dictionary components active.

291
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:34,800
And Lo and behold,
it's way better.

292
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:36,680
So we had a paper
last year, which

293
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:38,810
was just like here's
a bunch of them where

294
00:13:38,810 --> 00:13:41,503
we fit about 30 million
features on Claude 3

295
00:13:41,503 --> 00:13:43,170
Sonnet on the middle
layer of the model.

296
00:13:43,170 --> 00:13:45,470
So you just say,
what are the atoms

297
00:13:45,470 --> 00:13:48,440
of computation or representation
inside that model?

298
00:13:48,440 --> 00:13:52,850
This was one of my favorites,
where this linear combination

299
00:13:52,850 --> 00:13:55,040
is present, or it's like
a dot product with vector

300
00:13:55,040 --> 00:13:59,030
is large when the input is
about the Golden Gate Bridge.

301
00:13:59,030 --> 00:14:01,850
And that is true if it
is an explicit mention

302
00:14:01,850 --> 00:14:04,590
of the Golden Gate Bridge
in English on the left.

303
00:14:04,590 --> 00:14:08,480
Also, true if it's translated
into another language.

304
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:11,240
Also, true if it's an image
of the Golden Gate Bridge.

305
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:14,640
Also, true it turns out if
it's an indirect mention.

306
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:20,390
So you're like, I was driving
from San Francisco to Marin,

307
00:14:20,390 --> 00:14:22,620
which you crossed
the bridge to do.

308
00:14:22,620 --> 00:14:25,340
And the same feature
is active there.

309
00:14:25,340 --> 00:14:28,400
So it's some relatively general
concept also in San Francisco

310
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:30,575
landmarks, et cetera.

311
00:14:30,575 --> 00:14:32,700
So these combinations of
neurons are interpretable.

312
00:14:32,700 --> 00:14:33,700
We were happy with this.

313
00:14:33,700 --> 00:14:35,870
There's things that are
more abstract notions

314
00:14:35,870 --> 00:14:36,900
of inner conflict.

315
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:40,010
There was a feature
for bugs in code

316
00:14:40,010 --> 00:14:43,500
that fired on small
bugs like division

317
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:47,640
by zeros or typos or something
in many different programming

318
00:14:47,640 --> 00:14:48,140
languages.

319
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:49,682
If you suppressed
it, the model would

320
00:14:49,682 --> 00:14:50,860
act like there wasn't a bug.

321
00:14:50,860 --> 00:14:53,520
If you activated it, the model
would give you a traceback

322
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:54,640
as if there were a bug.

323
00:14:54,640 --> 00:14:59,170
And so it had this
general properties.

324
00:14:59,170 --> 00:15:01,780
But there was something very
unsatisfying about this,

325
00:15:01,780 --> 00:15:06,600
which is like, how does it know
it's the Golden Gate bridge?

326
00:15:06,600 --> 00:15:10,560
And so what?

327
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:12,760
What does it do with
that information?

328
00:15:12,760 --> 00:15:15,640
So even if you manage to piece
apart the representations,

329
00:15:15,640 --> 00:15:17,200
that's just a cross section.

330
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:18,910
It doesn't give you
the why and the how.

331
00:15:18,910 --> 00:15:20,520
It just gives you what.

332
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:24,930
And so we wanted to
find ways of connecting

333
00:15:24,930 --> 00:15:25,930
these features together.

334
00:15:25,930 --> 00:15:27,450
So you start from
the input words

335
00:15:27,450 --> 00:15:30,360
and you're going to process
these to higher order

336
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:31,090
representations.

337
00:15:31,090 --> 00:15:32,632
And eventually it
will say something.

338
00:15:32,632 --> 00:15:34,095
And trying to trace
that through--

339
00:15:34,095 --> 00:15:40,690
[INAUDIBLE] through
optimization.

340
00:15:40,690 --> 00:15:43,850
So very concretely,
you have a matrix,

341
00:15:43,850 --> 00:15:48,290
which is like you take a billion
examples of activation vectors,

342
00:15:48,290 --> 00:15:50,270
and then you've
got D model here.

343
00:15:50,270 --> 00:15:52,180
That's a matrix.

344
00:15:52,180 --> 00:15:58,870
And you try to factorize that
matrix into a product of a fixed

345
00:15:58,870 --> 00:16:02,620
dictionary of atoms times a
sparse matrix of which atoms

346
00:16:02,620 --> 00:16:04,820
are present in which example.

347
00:16:04,820 --> 00:16:06,650
And you just have an
objective function,

348
00:16:06,650 --> 00:16:10,360
which is this should reconstruct
the data and some L1 penalty

349
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:12,790
to encourage sparsity.

350
00:16:12,790 --> 00:16:14,200
[INAUDIBLE]

351
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:15,710

352
00:16:15,710 --> 00:16:17,740
It's a joint
optimization problem.

353
00:16:17,740 --> 00:16:22,030
Yeah, that's right,
that's right.

354
00:16:22,030 --> 00:16:24,560
We tried being
clever about this.

355
00:16:24,560 --> 00:16:26,620
There's a beautiful,
rich literature

356
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:28,210
on dictionary learning.

357
00:16:28,210 --> 00:16:31,400
But after a few months of
that the bitter lesson got us.

358
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:35,050
And it turned out that you could
just use a one layer sparse

359
00:16:35,050 --> 00:16:37,810
autoencoder, which you can put
in [INAUDIBLE] and then just

360
00:16:37,810 --> 00:16:39,230
train on your GPUs.

361
00:16:39,230 --> 00:16:41,930
And like that scaling was more
important than being clever

362
00:16:41,930 --> 00:16:42,780
yet again.

363
00:16:42,780 --> 00:16:43,800
So that's what it is.

364
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:46,630
It's a sparse autoencoder.

365
00:16:46,630 --> 00:16:47,130
All right.

366
00:16:47,130 --> 00:16:53,390
So here's a prompt.

367
00:16:53,390 --> 00:16:57,770
So the capital of the state
containing Dallas is Austin.

368
00:16:57,770 --> 00:16:59,540
This is true.

369
00:16:59,540 --> 00:17:04,969
That's because the state
containing Dallas is Texas.

370
00:17:04,969 --> 00:17:05,729
Good.

371
00:17:05,729 --> 00:17:07,560
Did the model do that?

372
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:09,060
Was it like Texas.

373
00:17:09,060 --> 00:17:10,630
So Austin or is it like--

374
00:17:10,630 --> 00:17:12,630
I don't know-- it's seen
a lot of training data.

375
00:17:12,630 --> 00:17:14,190
Was this just in it?

376
00:17:14,190 --> 00:17:16,109
And it's just like
reciting the answer.

377
00:17:16,109 --> 00:17:18,025
People-- there's a lot
of eval contamination.

378
00:17:18,025 --> 00:17:20,400
A lot of [INAUDIBLE] made it
into people's training sets.

379
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:21,390
So you get high scores.

380
00:17:21,390 --> 00:17:23,430
Well, just knew the
answer to that literally,

381
00:17:23,430 --> 00:17:24,388
because it had seen it.

382
00:17:24,388 --> 00:17:26,800
So did it literally see
this before or is it

383
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:28,300
kind of thinking
Texas like you did?

384
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:34,160

385
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:35,460
It's thinking about Texas.

386
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:38,760
So I'll tell you, this
is like a cartoon.

387
00:17:38,760 --> 00:17:43,020
And we can slowly
through this pick,

388
00:17:43,020 --> 00:17:46,090
break away these abstractions
to literally what we did.

389
00:17:46,090 --> 00:17:48,180
But I'll start by saying
that you should think

390
00:17:48,180 --> 00:17:54,520
of each of these as bundles
of features which are again,

391
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:58,120
atoms we learned through
an optimization process.

392
00:17:58,120 --> 00:18:00,480
We label the role
of each feature

393
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:02,940
by looking at when
it's active and trying

394
00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:05,970
to see if we can
describe in language

395
00:18:05,970 --> 00:18:07,600
what that feature is doing.

396
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:09,600
And then the
connections between them

397
00:18:09,600 --> 00:18:11,730
are actually direct
causal connections

398
00:18:11,730 --> 00:18:15,300
as the model processes
this in a forward pass.

399
00:18:15,300 --> 00:18:19,150
And so what we do is we break
apart the model into pieces.

400
00:18:19,150 --> 00:18:22,300
We ask which pieces are active,
can we interpret them SEPARATELY

401
00:18:22,300 --> 00:18:24,250
And then how does it flow.

402
00:18:24,250 --> 00:18:27,270
And so in this case, we found
a bunch of features related

403
00:18:27,270 --> 00:18:29,340
to capitals, a bunch
related to states

404
00:18:29,340 --> 00:18:32,220
in the abstract, a
bunch related to Dallas.

405
00:18:32,220 --> 00:18:33,900
We found some features
that were-- you

406
00:18:33,900 --> 00:18:35,655
can think of as motor neurons.

407
00:18:35,655 --> 00:18:37,030
They make the
model do something.

408
00:18:37,030 --> 00:18:39,197
In this case, they make it
say the name of a capital

409
00:18:39,197 --> 00:18:41,620
like they make it say a bunch
of state capitals or country

410
00:18:41,620 --> 00:18:42,980
capitals or something.

411
00:18:42,980 --> 00:18:44,830
So that's a start.

412
00:18:44,830 --> 00:18:46,280
But also it has to get it right.

413
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:51,920
And so there's some mapping
from Dallas into Texas.

414
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:53,720
And there's a pile
of features there.

415
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:58,700
Some are discussions
of Texas politics.

416
00:18:58,700 --> 00:19:02,710
Some are like phrases like,
everything's bigger in Texas.

417
00:19:02,710 --> 00:19:04,910
And once you have
Texas in a capital,

418
00:19:04,910 --> 00:19:07,990
you get saying
Austin in particular.

419
00:19:07,990 --> 00:19:09,490
And if you're saying
a state capital

420
00:19:09,490 --> 00:19:11,282
and also you're saying
Austin, what you get

421
00:19:11,282 --> 00:19:12,422
is like Austin coming out.

422
00:19:12,422 --> 00:19:14,380
There's some interesting
straight lines though,

423
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:17,863
so Texas also feeds
into Austin directly.

424
00:19:17,863 --> 00:19:19,780
If you're thinking about
Texas, you might just

425
00:19:19,780 --> 00:19:20,822
be thinking about Austin.

426
00:19:20,822 --> 00:19:23,500

427
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:28,120
And so I'll get into more
of how we build this.

428
00:19:28,120 --> 00:19:32,470
But this gives you
a picture in terms

429
00:19:32,470 --> 00:19:35,710
of these atoms or dictionary
elements we've learned.

430
00:19:35,710 --> 00:19:38,210
And you might want to
check that they make sense.

431
00:19:38,210 --> 00:19:40,460
So then you can do interventions
on the model deleting

432
00:19:40,460 --> 00:19:41,085
pieces of this.

433
00:19:41,085 --> 00:19:42,752
In neuroscience these
would be ablations

434
00:19:42,752 --> 00:19:43,800
of neurons or something.

435
00:19:43,800 --> 00:19:45,770
And then you see if
the output of the model

436
00:19:45,770 --> 00:19:49,010
changes as you would expect.

437
00:19:49,010 --> 00:19:52,940
Someone on Zoom asked words that
also frequently appear together

438
00:19:52,940 --> 00:19:56,340
in internet context, suggesting
simple statistical correlations.

439
00:19:56,340 --> 00:20:01,400
So is this a way of
overcomplicating things?

440
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:03,500
I think that people have
found that transformers

441
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:06,840
have outperformed
[INAUDIBLE] for most tasks.

442
00:20:06,840 --> 00:20:13,403
So Houston also occurs with
Dallas and the training data.

443
00:20:13,403 --> 00:20:14,820
And the model
doesn't say Houston.

444
00:20:14,820 --> 00:20:16,980
So it must be using
the capital and Dallas.

445
00:20:16,980 --> 00:20:19,640
In this case, I think
you could say maybe

446
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:22,250
if you just had
capital and Dallas,

447
00:20:22,250 --> 00:20:26,130
then it would say Austin.

448
00:20:26,130 --> 00:20:27,830
And that's actually
true, I think.

449
00:20:27,830 --> 00:20:29,940
Some of these edges
are pretty weak.

450
00:20:29,940 --> 00:20:33,650
And it turns out you could
just say the capital,

451
00:20:33,650 --> 00:20:35,280
you make something
ungrammatical,

452
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:39,588
just like the capital of Dallas
is and it will say Austin.

453
00:20:39,588 --> 00:20:41,130
And that's an
interesting thing where

454
00:20:41,130 --> 00:20:43,450
I think you look at the
graph and this edge is weak,

455
00:20:43,450 --> 00:20:45,075
and it indicates that
actually maybe it

456
00:20:45,075 --> 00:20:46,860
is just if you have
capital in Dallas

457
00:20:46,860 --> 00:20:48,880
and proximity, you
might get Austin,

458
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:51,100
which is then causally true.

459
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:51,600
Yes.

460
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:54,190
[INAUDIBLE]

461
00:20:54,190 --> 00:20:59,790

462
00:20:59,790 --> 00:21:02,640
I mean, the capital is
a neural network layer

463
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:03,520
and the bottom layer.

464
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:04,530
Yeah.

465
00:21:04,530 --> 00:21:06,060
My understanding is correct?

466
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:06,630
Yeah.

467
00:21:06,630 --> 00:21:10,000
So this is the flow through
layers of the model.

468
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:10,500
OK.

469
00:21:10,500 --> 00:21:11,583
Upstairs.

470
00:21:11,583 --> 00:21:13,000
And we've done
some grouping here.

471
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:18,513
So I will show in more detail.

472
00:21:18,513 --> 00:21:20,680
Let me actually see what
we're going to get to that.

473
00:21:20,680 --> 00:21:20,940
Yeah.

474
00:21:20,940 --> 00:21:23,273
So I'm going to give you a
lot more detail in just about

475
00:21:23,273 --> 00:21:25,320
a slide on the
technical side Yes.

476
00:21:25,320 --> 00:21:26,250
[INAUDIBLE]

477
00:21:26,250 --> 00:21:30,960

478
00:21:30,960 --> 00:21:35,180
We did a scan where we trained
dictionaries of different sizes.

479
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:43,150
And we-- you get some trade off
of obviously, the compute you

480
00:21:43,150 --> 00:21:49,370
spend and the amount or the
accuracy of the approximation.

481
00:21:49,370 --> 00:21:51,737
These are supposed to
reconstruct the activations.

482
00:21:51,737 --> 00:21:52,820
How well does that happen?

483
00:21:52,820 --> 00:21:54,890
The bigger the dictionary
the better it does.

484
00:21:54,890 --> 00:21:57,525
Also, the denser it is
the less sparse it is,

485
00:21:57,525 --> 00:21:58,400
the better that does.

486
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:00,500
But you pay some price on
interpretability at some point.

487
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:02,000
So we did a bunch
of sweeps and then

488
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,400
we picked something
that seems good enough.

489
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:05,750
There's still a bunch of errors.

490
00:22:05,750 --> 00:22:09,150
And I'll show you later
how those show up to--

491
00:22:09,150 --> 00:22:12,890
I mean that we can't
explain a lot of things.

492
00:22:12,890 --> 00:22:13,390
OK.

493
00:22:13,390 --> 00:22:15,760
I'm going to go on.

494
00:22:15,760 --> 00:22:22,030
So yeah, literally
what we do here

495
00:22:22,030 --> 00:22:24,410
is train this sparse
replacement model.

496
00:22:24,410 --> 00:22:27,112
So the basic idea is you have
a model of residual stream

497
00:22:27,112 --> 00:22:28,070
go through [INAUDIBLE].

498
00:22:28,070 --> 00:22:30,220
We're going to forget
about attention right now.

499
00:22:30,220 --> 00:22:31,970
And we're going to try
to approximate that

500
00:22:31,970 --> 00:22:33,690
with these cross-layer
transcoders.

501
00:22:33,690 --> 00:22:35,190
And so what do I mean by that?

502
00:22:35,190 --> 00:22:38,130
So transcoder is something
that moves information.

503
00:22:38,130 --> 00:22:40,130
So it emulates the MLPs.

504
00:22:40,130 --> 00:22:43,460
But the cross layer is
this ensemble of them

505
00:22:43,460 --> 00:22:44,908
emulates all the MLPs.

506
00:22:44,908 --> 00:22:47,450
There's an architecture called
DenseNet from a few years ago,

507
00:22:47,450 --> 00:22:49,920
where every layer writes
to every subsequent layer.

508
00:22:49,920 --> 00:22:51,060
And this is like that.

509
00:22:51,060 --> 00:22:55,490
So the basic idea is that
this ensemble of CLTs

510
00:22:55,490 --> 00:22:59,780
have to take in the inputs of
all the MLPs stack together

511
00:22:59,780 --> 00:23:03,050
and produce the vector of
all of their outputs at once.

512
00:23:03,050 --> 00:23:06,770
And a reason to do this is like
there's no particular reason

513
00:23:06,770 --> 00:23:08,990
to think that the atomic
units of computation

514
00:23:08,990 --> 00:23:10,500
have to live in one layer.

515
00:23:10,500 --> 00:23:12,720
If there's two consecutive
layers in a deep model,

516
00:23:12,720 --> 00:23:14,250
those neurons are
almost interchangeable.

517
00:23:14,250 --> 00:23:15,210
People have done experiments.

518
00:23:15,210 --> 00:23:17,418
You can actually swap the
order of transformer layers

519
00:23:17,418 --> 00:23:19,140
without damaging
performance that much,

520
00:23:19,140 --> 00:23:21,920
which means that indexing that
hard on the existing layer

521
00:23:21,920 --> 00:23:23,310
is maybe unwise.

522
00:23:23,310 --> 00:23:25,730
So we just say, OK, these
can skip to the end.

523
00:23:25,730 --> 00:23:27,890
This ends up making the
interpretability easier

524
00:23:27,890 --> 00:23:29,963
because sometimes it's
just bigram statistics.

525
00:23:29,963 --> 00:23:30,630
I see this word.

526
00:23:30,630 --> 00:23:34,388
I say that word that's
evident at layer 1,

527
00:23:34,388 --> 00:23:36,680
but you have to keep propagating
it all the way through

528
00:23:36,680 --> 00:23:37,560
to get it out.

529
00:23:37,560 --> 00:23:39,380
And so here we
could make that be

530
00:23:39,380 --> 00:23:42,770
one feature instead
of dozens of features

531
00:23:42,770 --> 00:23:45,320
in consecutive
layers interacting.

532
00:23:45,320 --> 00:23:47,550
And then we just train
that optimization.

533
00:23:47,550 --> 00:23:50,073
You've got a loss on accuracy
and a loss on sparsity.

534
00:23:50,073 --> 00:23:54,320

535
00:23:54,320 --> 00:24:01,340
And so here we replaced the
neurons with these features.

536
00:24:01,340 --> 00:24:03,630
We used just the attention
from the base models.

537
00:24:03,630 --> 00:24:05,130
We do not try to
explain attention.

538
00:24:05,130 --> 00:24:06,240
We just flow through it.

539
00:24:06,240 --> 00:24:09,980
But we try to explain
what the MLPs are doing.

540
00:24:09,980 --> 00:24:12,350
And now instead of
the neurons, which

541
00:24:12,350 --> 00:24:14,428
are of uninterpretable
like on the left here,

542
00:24:14,428 --> 00:24:16,470
we have stuff that makes
more sense on the right.

543
00:24:16,470 --> 00:24:20,840
This is say, a capital
feature, and I think

544
00:24:20,840 --> 00:24:22,080
it's probably more specific.

545
00:24:22,080 --> 00:24:29,025
So this is like in these literal
state to capital mappings.

546
00:24:29,025 --> 00:24:29,525
Yeah.

547
00:24:29,525 --> 00:24:31,380
[INAUDIBLE]

548
00:24:31,380 --> 00:24:41,880

549
00:24:41,880 --> 00:24:43,890
So I don't know.

550
00:24:43,890 --> 00:24:47,580
Reasonable, I'd
say it's expedient.

551
00:24:47,580 --> 00:24:50,940
It loses a lot.

552
00:24:50,940 --> 00:24:53,290
I think that from a practical
perspective, though,

553
00:24:53,290 --> 00:24:58,320
if you want to model the
action of the attention layer

554
00:24:58,320 --> 00:25:00,760
